.TH MITOS-UTILS-INTEGRATION 7 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS Miscellany"
.SH NAME
mitos\-utils\-integration \- using mitos\-utils from other MITOS projects
.SH DESCRIPTION
mitos\-utils is usable two ways by another MITOS crate: as an
\fBin\-process library\fR (call a function, no subprocess involved) or
as \fBstandalone executables\fR invoked as subprocesses. Every
applet's logic is a plain function,
.IR "mitos_utils::applets::<name>::run(args)" ,
and each standalone binary under
.I src/bin/
is a five\-line wrapper around that exact same function \(em see
.BR mitos\-box (1)
for the single\-binary\-many\-names variant of the same idea.
.PP
The in\-process route matters today specifically because mitosOS
doesn't have \fBfork\fR/\fBexec\fR yet: linking mitos\-utils in and
calling straight into
.I applets::*
is currently the only way a sibling hosted\-std MITOS project (such
as mitos\-shell) can use this crate's logic at all.
.PP
The full function\-by\-function API reference \(em every
.I common::
and
.I applets::
item, its signature, and what it's for \(em lives in
.I docs/integration.md
in the mitos\-utils source tree, along with the subprocess contract
(exit codes, stdout/stderr conventions, environment variables) for
once mitosOS can actually spawn processes, and the consolidated list
of what the kernel still needs to expose (\fBfork\fR/\fBexec\fR, a
.I /proc\-like
pseudo\-filesystem,
.BR statvfs (2),
a mount table, a kernel log read syscall, a passwd/group database).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-security (7)
